{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/730307-the-god-machine-in-requiem","id":730307,"title":"The God-Machine in Requiem","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-26T16:30:26-07:00","id":730307,"title":"The God-Machine in Requiem","content":"When I first heard about the 2e version of the WoD, I was under the impression that the God-Machine was intended to be a spine element that drew the main lines together. After digging through VtR and briefly flipping through WtF, that doesn't seem to be the case. I'm fine with that. It's a neat idea but one that seems best suited for a Demon game. I don't have much interest in fitting it into my Vampire game.<br /> <br /> That said, has anyone used it as a plot point in a VtR game? Also, for those who have been around the game a bit longer than me, was GM intended to be sweeping cross-book plot glue, or did it just happen to be the story choice they made for the rules update?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T16:40:06-07:00","id":730371,"title":"","content":"I once used the God-Machine based Covenant from Danse Macabre in a one-shot game about the God-Machine. Other than that, not really.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T17:19:51-07:00","id":730468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/730307-the-god-machine-in-requiem#post730307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, has anyone used it as a plot point in a VtR game? Also, for those who have been around the game a bit longer than me, was GM intended to be sweeping cross-book plot glue, or did it just happen to be the story choice they made for the rules update?</div> </div> </div> </div>It was part of the chronicle book setup, from before CCP gave the go-ahead to call the new rules a second edition; it was just that the mortal/blue-book line was going to have a signature chronicle antagonist that was the same antagonist from Demon, just viewed from a different perspective. The God-Machine was meant to fill the same role as the strix or the idigam: a fancy antagonist that doubled as an obvious, convenient answer for people who needed something to build a story around. (I believe they also batted around the idea of a Mothman Chronicle for the bluebooks before settling on the GMC.)<br /> <br /> So no, the God-Machine wasn't supposed to be a big fixture in Vampire any more than the strix are a big fixture in Demon. It's a thing in the World of Darkness, but so are the True Fae and the Seers of the Throne and all that. They're just not very Vampire-y, so Vampire books don't talk about them that much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T17:51:18-07:00","id":730531,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/730307-the-god-machine-in-requiem?p=730468#post730468\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It was part of the chronicle book setup, from before CCP gave the go-ahead to call the new rules a second edition; it was just that the mortal/blue-book line was going to have a signature chronicle antagonist that was the same antagonist from Demon, just viewed from a different perspective. The God-Machine was meant to fill the same role as the strix or the idigam: a fancy antagonist that doubled as an obvious, convenient answer for people who needed something to build a story around. (I believe they also batted around the idea of a Mothman Chronicle for the bluebooks before settling on the GMC.)<br /> <br /> So no, the God-Machine wasn't supposed to be a big fixture in Vampire any more than the strix are a big fixture in Demon. It's a thing in the World of Darkness, but so are the True Fae and the Seers of the Throne and all that. They're just not very Vampire-y, so Vampire books don't talk about them that much.</div> </div> </div> </div>That makes perfect sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T22:17:17-07:00","id":731270,"title":"","content":"Yeah, there's been the occasional nod or throwaway line, with the aforementioned Danse Macabre Covenant as the biggest most overt tie, but by and large it's an oddity of an unrelated game line, and an especially optional one at that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage the Awakening 2e Communal Homebrew Threads:<br /> - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966394-perils-of-the-path-post-your-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">Perils of the Path</a> - Other homebrew threads linked in the OP!<br /> <br /> Join us at the unofficial IRC channels on.irc.sorcery.net at #exalted and now #theonyxpath!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/451-kirby-jerusalem","name":"Kirby Jerusalem","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar451_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-27T19:47:31-07:00","id":733725,"title":"","content":"I actually have this as a big chunk of my current mixed book session since we have a very mixed group in my game (Vampire, Mage, and Changeling). I added in the God Machine because I had a diverse group, and the players in my game really like heavy investigation and enigma like stories. I threw in a Demon who is an NPC guide while the team is in New York looking to take out an over all threat posed by the Machine.<br /> <br /> To be honest though, unless you really want to go for a long haul, I really had to wrangle it into the story to give the characters reason. The vampire was easy, the thing is taking in potential food. Mage? He just wants to investigate it and see if there is anything he can learn from it (Tech based mage) and the Changeling sees it as a disruption. Besides notoriety in the game for taking down a branch of the Machine, I did bribe them with a bit of money via the Demon.<br /> <br /> Note my players as I said like a lot of enigma based stories and lead them into dead ends sometimes, and they have to actually think out what is happening. If you do not have players like this, I have a feeling they would have a tough time with it. Ran solo with mortals, sure, but adding in the additional races, you need to really give them incentive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8027,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8027-calconstantine","user":"CalConstantine","date":". 10-27-2015, 10:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8027-calconstantine","name":"CalConstantine","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-27T21:45:02-07:00","id":733900,"title":"","content":"For the record, I don't intend to mix GM into my game. It seems like an ill fit for Vampire and particularly the Vampire game I'm planning. OTOH, it could make a decent substitute for the presence of the Weaver and the Technocracy in the oWoD (though I'm not sure how closely Forsaken and Awakening resemble the originals). I was just curious to see if and how anyone else incorporated it into their VtR chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","user":"dxanders","date":". 10-28-2015, 12:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T00:33:10-07:00","id":734058,"title":"","content":"The San Francisco write-up in VtR 2e mentions a Wyrm's Nest somewhere in Stanford. In my setting, that Wyrm's Nest turned out to be Infrastructure. It was a very weird place where space and time did not act as one would expect, making it rather perilous. The God-Machine was using it to have one of his Angels experiment on Vampires from all across history. He only needed one more to complete his plot and he'd let the other PCs go free, but the players didn't take that bait.<br /> <br /> I vaguely recall one of the interpretations for the G-M in GMC is that it can only account for mortals in its plans, which leaves it to generally ignoring supernaturals. The point of the Infrastructure was to try and let the G-M adapt to include Vampires in its plans, too.<br /> <br /> I think it was a pretty good plot line for Ordo Dracul characters and their allies.<br /> <br /> So that's how I integrated some G-M into my campaign. Hope that was interesting for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-30T14:41:39-07:00","id":742942,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/730307-the-god-machine-in-requiem?p=734058#post734058\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The San Francisco write-up in VtR 2e mentions a Wyrm's Nest somewhere in Stanford. In my setting, that Wyrm's Nest turned out to be Infrastructure. It was a very weird place where space and time did not act as one would expect, making it rather perilous. The God-Machine was using it to have one of his Angels experiment on Vampires from all across history. He only needed one more to complete his plot and he'd let the other PCs go free, but the players didn't take that bait.<br /> <br /> I vaguely recall one of the interpretations for the G-M in GMC is that it can only account for mortals in its plans, which leaves it to generally ignoring supernaturals. The point of the Infrastructure was to try and let the G-M adapt to include Vampires in its plans, too.<br /> <br /> I think it was a pretty good plot line for Ordo Dracul characters and their allies.<br /> <br /> So that's how I integrated some G-M into my campaign. Hope that was interesting for you.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> That actually sounds really cool, and the inquisitive mission statement of the Dragons seem like a good match for incorporating the God-Machine into a Vampire game.<br /> <br /> I still have a lot of consternation about crossover gaming in the WoD. I think a lot of that is from my experience in cWoD and the fact that, out of all of the current day games, VtR seems the most tailored towards establishing an insular setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-30T15:19:18-07:00","id":743024,"title":"","content":"Yeah. I tend to feel that a Vampire focus benefits best from crossover when it's sparse and distant. Vampires are some of the most practical gribblies of the World of Darkness in my eyes. They're not blind to sharing the world with foreign shadows, but they believe good fences make good neighbors. They put up some cautionary defenses in case trouble starts, and then as long as trouble doesn't start and the darkness stays out of their business, they're happy to stay out of its business in turn. (Until some clever asshole neonate or dragon decides to try to drag the darkness in as an X-factor weapon against rivals, anyway.)<br /> <br /> As long as those fences stay up (and I think they stay up pretty well in the long run), crossover elements take the form of urban legends, unanticipated dangers and complicating factors. The monster in the cave isn't a beast for the vampire to slay, but a problem to avoid when seeking cover from an enemy. A mage might get unexpectedly caught up in the harrowing of a spirit from the Hisil, and that'll spark an adventure to figure out the nature of the spirit and its motives for running wild, and then to seek to control, banish, destroy or transform it. The vampire gets unexpectedly caught up in a bizarre spirit harrowing, and that's just a bad day to remind you that unexpected shit happens to complicate your Requiem. Maybe you end up having to cash in some favors to recover that put you in a spot later, or somebody dies in the confusion and their death is pinned on a Carthian assassin. But if the spirit doesn't keep following the vampire, the vampire just doesn't go back to that place. There's no need to learn what its deal is or where it came from. Mark off the lost territory on the map and go spend your time somewhere safer, around people who can more easily be manipulated. Let some other sap play hero.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-30T15:25:08-07:00","id":743030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/730307-the-god-machine-in-requiem?p=743024#post743024\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah. I tend to feel that a Vampire focus benefits best from crossover when it's sparse and distant. Vampires are some of the most practical gribblies of the World of Darkness in my eyes. They're not blind to sharing the world with foreign shadows, but they believe good fences make good neighbors. They put up some cautionary defenses in case trouble starts, and then as long as trouble doesn't start and the darkness stays out of their business, they're happy to stay out of its business in turn. (Until some clever asshole neonate or dragon decides to try to drag the darkness in as an X-factor weapon against rivals, anyway.)<br /> <br /> As long as those fences stay up (and I think they stay up pretty well in the long run), crossover elements take the form of urban legends, unanticipated dangers and complicating factors. The monster in the cave isn't a beast for the vampire to slay, but a problem to avoid when seeking cover from an enemy. A mage might get unexpectedly caught up in the harrowing of a spirit from the Hisil, and that'll spark an adventure to figure out the nature of the spirit and its motives for running wild, and then to seek to control, banish, destroy or transform it. The vampire gets unexpectedly caught up in a bizarre spirit harrowing, and that's just a bad day to remind you that unexpected shit happens to complicate your Requiem. Maybe you end up having to cash in some favors to recover that put you in a spot later, or somebody dies in the confusion and their death is pinned on a Carthian assassin. But if the spirit doesn't keep following the vampire, the vampire just doesn't go back to that place. There's no need to learn what its deal is or where it came from. Mark off the lost territory on the map and go spend your time somewhere safer, around people who can more easily be manipulated. Let some other sap play hero.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, I think the limits they put on the kindred cleverly facilitates that, and it's one of the parts of the game that really appeals to me. The demands of travel make leaving your city (or even the neighborhoods you're comfortable with) a risk most vampires would rather avoid, and you're severely limited by the necessity of feeding, the restrictions on your feeding habits placed by the territorial nature of your peers, and the limited window in which you're awake.<br /> <br /> It's an environment that breeds xenophobia and complacency to anything outside the kindred power structure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-30T15:25:55-07:00","id":743032,"title":"","content":"And if all else fails, shove the Ordo Dracul into it. They like that sort of shit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-30T15:58:40-07:00","id":743136,"title":"","content":"One of the several explanations for VII in the Requiem core book refers directly to the God-Machine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-31T02:45:45-07:00","id":744062,"title":"","content":"I'd go with the Architects of the Monolith and infrastructure for this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-31T06:23:20-07:00","id":744319,"title":"","content":"Interestingly, to me at least, Theban Sorcery actually makes sense as a kind of Occult Matrix output. Theban is holy magic that requires unique sacrifices to work, and we know that sacrifice is how the God Machine generates the essence to perform its techno-miracles (at least for angels). And Theban generally is explained as channeling the power of God, Amoniel, Vahishtael, or a different angel (at least since the LS book came out), and Theban rituals are learned in a similar manner to how demons learn their Embeds. <br /> <br /> So, if you want to bring in the God Machine into Requiem, I'd highly recommend actually going for making Theban Sorcerers (or maybe anyone with Lancea et Sanctum status, even non-sorcerers) be part of a unique Infrastructure. That means we could have angels hovering over vampiric shoulders, or demons seeking to destroy the local Lance (or subverting them somehow into providing aether for the Unchained). Something along those lines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-31T15:59:34-07:00","id":745294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/730307-the-god-machine-in-requiem?p=733900#post733900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the record, I don't intend to mix GM into my game. It seems like an ill fit for Vampire and particularly the Vampire game I'm planning. OTOH, it could make a decent substitute for the presence of the Weaver and the Technocracy in the oWoD (though I'm not sure how closely Forsaken and Awakening resemble the originals). I was just curious to see if and how anyone else incorporated it into their VtR chronicle.</div> </div> </div> </div>You could do it in a less overt way, too. In my current game, which is a Werewolf/Changeling 2E combined setting (so, not <b>Vampire</b>, but close enough to share a few details), one of the characters is a Wolf-Blooded detective. I'm about to pit her against a serial killer. Here are my initial ideas. Spoilers, for anyone who is in my Hunting Grounds: The Cascades game on RPOL (don't look or you'll ruin the game!):<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b>The Clockwork Killer</b><br /> A series of bizarre murders shakes this seemingly warm little mountain community. The bodies of victims are discovered with clock numbers carved into their faces, and clock-hands on a spike driven through the face where the nose used to be. Perhaps even more puzzling is the fact that each victim's heart has been surgically removed and replaced with an antique alarm clock. The clock's alarm goes off inside the person's chest (if not discovered before then) at the time indicated by the clock etched into their face. Unknown to investigators (at least at first), this is also the time the person died.<br /> <br /> The press dubs this murderer &quot;the Clockwork Killer,&quot; and his killing spree vexes the police. None of the victims seem to have any ties. His motives are strange, to say the least. It turns out that our killer, Franco Fawkes, is carrying out a mission of divine importance. An angel came to him in his dreams, upon wings like buzzsaws, speaking with a voice like a rattling alarm. The angel told Franco that the world was removed from God due to being out of sync with His divine calendar. Humanity was slipping further and further away, the world falling into damnation, because of its discord with the perfect harmony of God. The faithful, like Franco, must take action in order to restore the proper timing sequence and return the world to grace. Thus Franco targets those he views are out of step with time: the young who act older than they are, the old who act too immature, survivors of previous accidents that should have killed them. In removing their hearts, the seat of the soul, and replacing them with clocks that strike to match the moment of death, he sends the proper signals to God that those responsible for the mistiming of the world have been restored to the proper cycle.<br /> <br /> Is the so-called Clockwork Killer insane? Clearly. But is he touched by the (clock)hand of God? Perhaps an angel of the God-Machine did indeed visit him\u2014that's what I think happened. He's completely mortal but also influenced by the God-Machine. You could use him in any kind of chronicle, including <b>Vampire: The Requiem</b>. It could serve to unsettle even the undead, hinting at dark forces beyond Kindred conception. The implications are terrifying. Tell yourself he's just some psycho, but in the back of your mind you have to wonder....</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Actually, it occurs to me that the Clockwork Killer would work really well in a <b>Vampire: The Requiem </b>game. Maybe true vampires are a bit outside of his scope, but you know who'd be the prime candidates for divine excision? Ghouls. People sustained beyond the flow of time by the blood of monsters. Their very existence defies God's perfect timing for the universe. When he starts killing ghouls, the Kindred take notice. How is he finding them? Is it the work of a rival? Is the killer fed their coordinates in his dreams by an angel, or does he have superior investigative skills that allow him to pierce the veil of the Masquerade? Will you keep all your ghouls with you at all times, and will that be enough? If a player character's ghoul is killed, that's a perfect way to draw them personally into the story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":109,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","user":"Claire Redfield","date":". 10-31-2015, 07:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-31T16:50:06-07:00","id":745332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Claire Redfield</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/730307-the-god-machine-in-requiem?p=745294#post745294\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You could do it in a less overt way, too. In my current game, which is a Werewolf/Changeling 2E combined setting (so, not <b>Vampire</b>, but close enough to share a few details), one of the characters is a Wolf-Blooded detective. I'm about to pit her against a serial killer. Here are my initial ideas. Spoilers, for anyone who is in my Hunting Grounds: The Cascades game on RPOL (don't look or you'll ruin the game!):<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getE